{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEmx7s3QUtrnvthyzygB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the bug, and all 5 tests pass (`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`).\n\n`parse_price` in `inventory.py` now treats commas as thousands separators when the whole string looks like `1,234.50` or `1,234,567`. That means groups of exactly three digits after the commas, followed by a dot or another comma group. Everything else still goes through the old decimal-comma path, so `\"12,50\"`, `\"12.50\"` and `\"$3\"` behave as before. `total_value` is unchanged.\n\nA bare `\"1,234\"` (one comma group, no dot) is still read as a European decimal, giving `1.234`. The issue only calls a comma a thousands separator when a dot or another comma group follows, so I left that case alone.\n\nI added a regression test, `test_thousands_separator`, in `tests/test_inventory.py`. It covers `\"1,234.50\"` → `1234.5` and `\"$1,234,567\"` → `1234567.0`.\n\nI haven't committed anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1186,"cache_read_input_tokens":23922,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1186},"output_tokens":328,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4f77b73b-be43-444d-a403-0181109644be","uuid":"570703ee-458b-426e-a2e9-2201de4eb896","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:47:06.969Z","request_id":"req_011CgEmx7KouPGk4kkdHDV1i"}
